12.5 TL
(   [Problem Statement]: Prove that plane electromagnetic waves in free space are strictly transverse in nature \(i.e., both E and H have zero components along the direction of propagation\).) Tj
T*
(   [Detailed Step-by-Step Answer]:) Tj
T*
(   1. Definition of a Plane Wave:) Tj
T*
(   - Assume a uniform plane wave propagating along the +z direction.) Tj
T*
(   - Spatial derivatives with respect to x and y vanish: d/dx = 0, d/dy = 0.) Tj
T*
(   - Electric and magnetic fields are functions only of z and t:) Tj
T*
(   E = E_x\(z,t\) i + E_y\(z,t\) j + E_z\(z,t\) k) Tj
T*
(   H = H_x\(z,t\) i + H_y\(z,t\) j + H_z\(z,t\) k) Tj
T*
() Tj
T*
(   2. Transverse Nature of Electric Field E:) Tj
T*
(   - Apply Maxwell's first equation in free space \(Del dot E = 0\):) Tj
T*
(   \(dE_x/dx\) + \(dE_y/dy\) + \(dE_z/dz\) = 0.) Tj
T*
(   - Since dE_x/dx = 0 and dE_y/dy = 0, this leaves: dE_z / dz = 0.) Tj
T*
(   - Now consider the wave equation for the z-component: d^2E_z / dz^2 = mu_0*epsilon_0 * \(d^2E_z) Tj
T*
(   / dt^2\).) Tj
T*
(   - Since dE_z/dz = 0, it follows that d^2E_z/dt^2 = 0, meaning E_z can at most be a static,) Tj
T*
(   uniform field. For a propagating time-harmonic wave, E_z must be identically zero: E_z = 0.) Tj
T*
() Tj
T*
(   3. Transverse Nature of Magnetic Field H:) Tj
T*
(   - Apply Maxwell's second equation \(Del dot B = 0 => Del dot H = 0\):) Tj
T*
(   \(dH_x/dx\) + \(dH_y/dy\) + \(dH_z/dz\) = 0 => dH_z / dz = 0.) Tj
T*
(   - Similarly, from the wave equation for H_z, dynamic solutions require: H_z = 0.) Tj
T*
() Tj
T*
(   4. Conclusion:) Tj
T*
(   - Because E_z = 0 and H_z = 0, both electric and magnetic field vectors lie strictly in the) Tj
T*
(   transverse xy-plane, perpendicular to the direction of propagation \(+z\). Hence, plane EM waves) Tj
T*
(   are purely transverse.) Tj
T*
() Tj
T*
(--------------------------------------------------------------------------------) Tj
T*
() Tj
T*
