(   [Problem Statement]: Explain the physical significance of complex permittivity and loss tangent. For seawater \(epsilon_r = 80, sigma = 4.0 S/m\), calculate the loss tangent and determine if it acts as a conductor or dielectric at: \(a\) 10 kHz, \(b\) 10 GHz.) Tj
T*
(   [Detailed Step-by-Step Answer]:) Tj
T*
(   1. Complex Permittivity and Loss Tangent:) Tj
T*
(   - In Ampere-Maxwell's law: Del x H = \(sigma + j*omega*epsilon\) E = j*omega*epsilon_c * E.) Tj
T*
(   - Complex permittivity: epsilon_c = epsilon' - j*epsilon'', where epsilon' = epsilon \(energy) Tj
T*
(   storage\) and epsilon'' = sigma / omega \(dissipative ohmic loss\).) Tj
T*
(   - Loss tangent: tan\(delta\) = epsilon'' / epsilon' = sigma / \(omega * epsilon\) = sigma / \(2*pi*f) Tj
T*
(   * epsilon_r * epsilon_0\).) Tj
T*
(   - Classification criteria:) Tj
T*
(   * Good dielectric: tan\(delta\) << 0.1) Tj
T*
(   * Good conductor: tan\(delta\) >> 10) Tj
T*
(   * Quasi-conductor / lossy dielectric: 0.1 <= tan\(delta\) <= 10.) Tj
T*
() Tj
T*
(   2. \(a\) At f = 10 kHz \(10^4 Hz\):) Tj
T*
(   - omega = 2*pi * 10^4 = 6.283 x 10^4 rad/s.) Tj
T*
(   - epsilon = 80 * 8.854 x 10^-12 = 7.083 x 10^-10 F/m.) Tj
T*
(   - tan\(delta\) = 4.0 / \(6.283 x 10^4 * 7.083 x 10^-10\) = 4.0 / \(4.45 x 10^-5\) = 8.99 x 10^4 >>) Tj
T*
(   10.) Tj
T*
(   - Conclusion: At 10 kHz, seawater behaves as an outstanding conductor.) Tj
T*
() Tj
T*
(   3. \(b\) At f = 10 GHz \(10^10 Hz\):) Tj
T*
(   - omega = 2*pi * 10^10 = 6.283 x 10^10 rad/s.) Tj
T*
(   - tan\(delta\) = 4.0 / \(6.283 x 10^10 * 7.083 x 10^-10\) = 4.0 / 44.5 = 0.0899 < 0.1.) Tj
T*
(   - Conclusion: At 10 GHz, seawater transitions into a lossy dielectric.) Tj
T*
() Tj
T*
(--------------------------------------------------------------------------------) Tj
T*
() Tj
T*
